u ask them what kind of tone  is out the third the fourth that I don't  think about it because they learn it by  chance  for example they didn't learn the single  tone so knowing how to pronounce to  learn tones is quite important and  having said that in Chinese I would I  would say that one of the most important  things to do at the beginning of the  very beginning is to focus on pronounce  not only how words and vowels are  produced so tones and and as well as  consonants because there are retroflex  they're called retroflex consonants are  a little bit difficult and then you can  move on to other aspects of the language  well I would say first of all learning  as a whole try to learn it as a whole  you know but focusing pronunciation is  not is not a bad idea because once you  have you got the pronunciation right  everything is easier because grammar is  hmm I wouldn't make the mistake of  calling you know saying that Chinese  grammar is pretty straightforward at a  basic level it's pretty straightforward  when when you move on to intermediate  and advanced it's much more difficult  because Chinese has a different way of  conveying ideas so you have to  completely change the way you express  yourself even if the grammatical  structure is apparently simple but in to  get all of its a pronunciation first  obviously you still learn a language and  then you move on for example to you  create a bond between the sound and the  written language pinging use you know  the other step is try to stick to Pina  first the transliteration you know in  Latin alphabet because it's going to be  much easier and then after 2 or 3 months  you can start learning characters one  more thing then this goes for both  Chinese and Japanese is that the amount  of characters that you can actually  handwrite doesn't has nothing to do with  your fluency you can be fluent in  Chinese without even being able to read  characters if you if you wanted that to  happen but I suggest yet in you know  